## Typography

Two font families:

```css
font-family: var(--font-family);   /* Inter — body, UI, forms */
font-family: var(--font-display);  /* Playfair Display — editorial headlines */
```

Available sizes:

- `--font-xs`: `0.75rem`
- `--font-sm`: `0.8125rem`
- `--font-base`: `0.875rem`
- `--font-md`: `1rem`
- `--font-lg`: `1.125rem`
- `--font-xl`: `1.25rem`
- `--font-2xl`: `1.5rem`
- `--font-3xl`: `1.875rem`

Rules:

- Use Inter (var(--font-family)) for all app UI: dashboards, tables, forms, nav, settings.
- Use Playfair Display (var(--font-display)) only for landing page hero/section titles and marketing headings.
- Do not use Playfair Display inside authenticated app screens.
- Use compact type in dashboards, tables, forms, nav, and settings.
- Use `--font-xl` or smaller for most app page headings.

## App Shell

Desktop:

- Fixed left sidebar starts collapsed at `64px`
- Expanded sidebar width is `220px`
- Sidebar must be user-collapsible
- Main content uses the page's own layout; split workspace pages should fill available height

Mobile:

- Hide sidebar at `max-width: 768px`
- Show fixed bottom nav with app-level entries only
- Add bottom padding to main content so content is not hidden behind bottom nav
- Tables and wide rows must scroll inside wrappers, never push viewport width

## Product Flow

The authenticated app is property-first. Do not expose every data module as a top-level navigation item.

Top-level navigation:

- Dashboard
- Properties
- Timeline
- Settings

Hidden/internal routes that may still exist:

- Tenants
- Agreements
- Rent Ledger
- Cash Flow
- Expenses
- Maintenance
- Vendors

These hidden routes should not be brought back into the primary sidebar or mobile bottom nav unless the user explicitly changes the product direction.

Property workspace tabs:

- Overview
- Tenants
- Agreements
- Ledger
- Expenses
- Maintenance

Use the property workspace as the main place to manage property-specific records. Standalone pages can remain available for legacy/direct access, but they should not compete with Properties as the primary user flow.

Expenses and Maintenance relationship:

- Expenses is for financial expense records: taxes, utilities, insurance, and management fees.
- Maintenance is for repair/work-order management.
- Cash Flow and dashboard summaries may include maintenance cost in financial calculations.
- Do not show maintenance items inside the editable Expenses table; it creates a dead-end because maintenance has different fields and lifecycle.

## Components

### Buttons

Use existing classes:

- `.btn`
- `.btn-primary` — **inverse monochrome fill** (`--text-primary` bg, `--bg-primary` text)
- `.btn-secondary`
- `.btn-danger`
- `.btn-ghost`
- `.btn-outline`
- `.btn-sm`
- `.btn-icon`

Primary buttons use the monochrome accent:

```css
background: var(--text-primary);
color: var(--bg-primary);
```

Do not introduce bright colors for primary actions. The monochrome inverse accent is the singular style for primary interactive elements.

### Cards

Use `.card` for standard panels:

```css
background: var(--bg-secondary);
border: 1px solid var(--border);
border-radius: var(--radius-lg);   /* 24px */
padding: var(--space-lg);
```

Cards may use a subtle border or shadow change on hover. Avoid large scale transforms.

### Glass Surfaces

Use `.glass-card` or matching tokens for topbar/bottom nav:

```css
background: var(--glass-bg);
backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur);
border: 1px solid var(--glass-border);
box-shadow: var(--glass-shadow);
```

Glass should be subtle. In light theme, prefer clean white surfaces with subtle shadows over glassmorphism.

### Forms

Inputs, selects, and textareas are globally styled. Preserve:

- `background: var(--bg-tertiary)`
- `border: 1px solid var(--border-strong)`
- `border-radius: var(--radius-md)` (10px)
- visible focus ring

Use `.form-row` for two-column desktop forms that collapse to one column below 600px.

### Tables

Use `.data-table` and `.table-container` patterns.

Rules:

- Header cells are compact uppercase labels.
- Rows have subtle hover background.
- Avoid excessive padding on dense data views.
- Wrap wide tables in horizontal scroll containers on mobile.

### Badges

Use:

- `.badge-success`
- `.badge-warning`
- `.badge-danger`
- `.badge-info`
- `.badge-neutral`

Badges are small uppercase pills. Use semantic status only.

### Modals

Use the existing modal pattern:

- `.modal-overlay`
- `.modal-container`
- `.modal-sm`
- `.modal-md`
- `.modal-lg`

On mobile, modals behave like bottom sheets. Keep this behavior.

### Slide Panels

Use slide panels for detail editing. Preserve:

- right-side desktop panel
- full-height panel
- dark overlay
- rounded left corners
- scrollable content

### Selects and Toggles

- Custom selects use `cs-*` classes.
- Toggle groups use `.toggle-group`, `.toggle-btn`, `.toggle-btn-active`.
- Active toggle uses **monochrome inverse styling** (`--text-primary` bg, `--bg-primary` text).

## Landing Page

The landing page has its own `lp-*` namespace in `src/pages/Landing.css`.

Landing direction:

- Warm light canvas (`#f5f2f0`)
- White cards with subtle shadows
- Dark monochrome accent for CTAs and highlights
- Playfair Display for hero/section headings
- Inter for body text
- Soft ambient blobs with subtle neutral tint
- Product UI mockup in hero
- Stats strip, feature grid, testimonials, integrations, pricing, CTA, footer

Landing is always light-themed regardless of app theme setting.

Responsive behavior:

- Hero stacks below 1024px
- Nav turns into mobile menu below 768px
- Multi-column grids collapse to one column below 768px
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`

## Tech Stack Note

Tailwind CSS v4 is installed but the app primarily uses hand-authored CSS with design tokens. Tailwind utilities may appear in some components. Prefer design tokens and existing component classes over ad-hoc Tailwind utilities.

Framer Motion is available for animations. The `src/components/ui/` directory contains Aceternity-style effect components (glowing effects, sparkles, etc.) used on the landing page.

## Icons

Preferred icon library is `lucide-react`.

Rules:

- Do not use emoji as React UI icons.
- Icon-only buttons must have `title`, `aria-label`, or visible accessible text.
- Use familiar icons for actions.

## Motion

Use existing transition tokens:

- `--transition-fast`: `0.15s ease`
- `--transition-base`: `0.2s ease`
- `--transition-slow`: `0.3s ease`

Motion should clarify interaction, not decorate. Acceptable:

- fade in page content
- hover border/background changes
- slight `translateY(-1px)` on buttons
- slide panels and mobile sheets

Avoid heavy animations in data dashboards.

## Responsiveness

Required breakpoints:

- `768px`: mobile shell switch
- `600px`: form rows collapse
- `480px`: landing page compact spacing
- `400px`: very narrow dashboard stats become one column

Always verify:

- No horizontal body scroll
- Text does not overflow buttons or cards
- Tables scroll inside wrappers
- Fixed topbar, sidebar, and bottom nav do not cover content

## Files and Patterns to Reuse

Before creating new UI, look for existing patterns:

- Dashboard cards and stats: `src/pages/Dashboard.css`
- Property cards and detail tabs: `src/pages/Properties.css`
- Data tables: global `.data-table`
- Modal forms: `src/components/common/Modal.css`
- Slide panels: `src/components/common/SlidePanel.css`
- Toggles: `src/components/common/ToggleGroup.css`
- Selects: `src/components/common/CustomSelect.css`

Prefer extending these patterns over creating new visual systems.

## Forbidden Old Direction

Do not bring back:

- Teal palette
- Cinzel / Josefin Sans fonts
- Real-estate luxury brochure styling
- Beige/tan property brochure palettes
- Purple-blue gradient SaaS palettes
- Cool slate light theme (old: `#f8fafc`, `#f1f5f9`)

